      "content": "A constant humming noise can be a bit more concerning, and it's possible that a part has indeed died or a component has failed. It's like a lone, wandering goblin, causing trouble and making a racket – you can't quite pinpoint its source, but you know it's there.\n\nAs for the capacitor, that's a great place to start. A dead or faulty capacitor can cause a humming noise, and it's a relatively accessible component to check. However, be careful when working with electrical components, as they can be a bit like a mischievous imp, playing tricks on you if you're not careful. Make sure to follow proper safety protocols, like turning off the power to the unit and using insulated tools.\n\nIf you're comfortable with the process, you can try checking the capacitor, but if you're not sure or if the humming noise persists after checking, it's still best to leave it to a professional. They'll be able to diagnose the issue and replace the faulty component, just like a skilled goblin hunter tracking down a pesky goblin in the forest.",
